  "markdown_report": "The best first experiment remains **content-gated causal dilation inside NextItNet**, targeting stale interests and broad recommendations that miss recent purpose. Improvements remain unmeasured; the architecture changes context processing but does not guarantee forgetting.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Coherent recent interests motivate adaptive context selection. (2) Persistent older interactions motivate shorter-context controls. (3) Electronics’ 12.27% sampled ranking-error rate and −0.2595 lower-tail margin motivate contextual discrimination. Embedding scores near 0.5 do not support collapse regularization; sampled training margins do not establish validation gains.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) [NextItNet](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163) supplies causal residual convolutions. (2) [SKNet](https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06586) provides closer branch-selection precedent than vision-only [FADC](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05369), reducing novelty. (3) [SkipRec](https://cdn.aaai.org/ojs/16518/16518-13-20012-1-2-20210518.pdf) supports adaptive NextItNet computation, not demonstrated stale-context rejection. (4) [Recformer](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13731) and [sampled-softmax analysis](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf) motivate alternative representation and supervision changes without establishing benchmark gains.\n\nThe framework has three directions: **context → causal branch selection → recent purpose**; **representation → jointly learned text fusion → specific identity**; **supervision → multiple negatives → confusing candidates**. Missing compatibility and replacement-intent labels constrain every direction.\n\nScores are Originality / Potential / User alignment / Model alignment / Difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each dimension includes a strength (+) and limitation (−).\n\n- **A. Content-gated dilation: 4/7/8/6/5.** Originality: + causal adaptation; − established branch selection. Potential: + inspectable contextual control; − uncertain gains. User: + targets stale interests and broad-purpose mismatch; − compatibility unresolved. Model: + contextual discrimination; − separation gains unproven. Difficulty: + localized modification; − causal masking and controls require care.\n- **B. Joint title/description fusion: 4/7/7/5/6.** Originality: + constrained sharing; − established fusion. Potential: + sparse-item support; − weak text supervision. User: + functional distinctions; − duplicates may intensify. Model: + shared statistical strength; − noisy descriptions. Difficulty: + pooled encoder; − vocabulary and serialization work.\n- **C. Uniform multi-negative softmax: 3/6/5/8/3.** Originality: + task adaptation; − standard objective. Potential: + extensible; − sampling-sensitive. User: + specific-item competition; − stale context untreated. Model: + confusing-negative supervision; − false negatives. Difficulty: + localized loss change; − extra memory.\n\nSelect **A** at 0% progress, preserving the baseline loss and optimizer. For each block use `h′=h+Fbase(h)+αg(h)⊙[Fs(h)−Fbase(h)]`, with scalar `α=0` initially. This exactly preserves the baseline block at initialization; sigmoid bias alone does not. Gates use per-position channel features, never future/global sequence pooling. Residual states can retain distant information despite short-branch selection.\n\nPreregister baseline, shorter-window, static-mixture, and gated configurations, checkpoint schedule, stopping rule, and a practical macro-HR improvement threshold before training. Prioritize these controls within 43,200 GPU-seconds; reserve parameter-matched controls for a promising result. Charge failures and diagnostics, report throughput/parameters, and prefer simpler configurations on ties.\n\nCompare identical validation users/candidates. Report paired within-category bootstrap macro differences, 95% intervals, category differences, and discordant hits; one changed hit shifts macro HR by 0.00125. Intervals exclude seed/negative variability and selection optimism ([Cawley–Talbot](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html)). Gate shuffling and old-prefix perturbations are supporting diagnostics. Preserve causal masks, final-nonpadding readout, synchronized predictor code, and one checkpoint per category. Freeze selection before held-out evaluation; all search comparisons remain exploratory.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a zero-initialized, jointly trained correction that interpolates between existing and short-dilation causal residual updates inside the supplied NextItNet. Each category remains one network and one checkpoint. Compare against static mixing and shorter histories before attributing gains to content-dependent context selection.",
    "motivation": "The selected idea explicitly targets two user-facing failures: older isolated interactions persisting despite coherent recent behavior, and broadly relevant recommendations displacing specific matches to current purpose. It also targets the model-facing contextual discrimination problem suggested by Electronics’ 12.27% sampled wrong-ranking rate and −0.2595 lower-tail margin. These observations motivate the experiment but do not demonstrate its mechanism or predict HR@10 improvement. Duplication, compatibility, and replacement intent remain unobserved and unresolved.",
    "implementation_notes": "Extend the supplied corrected baseline; do not reconstruct missing host training or predictor code. For each original block h + Fbase(h), add Fs(h) with the same channel dimensions and kernel sizes but dilation 1 in each added convolution. Define h_new = h + Fbase(h) + alpha * sigmoid(Wg h + bg) * (Fs(h) - Fbase(h)); use one trainable scalar alpha per block, initialized to zero, and a channelwise pointwise gate. Initialize gate bias to zero and added weights using the baseline initialization convention. Zero alpha preserves baseline outputs exactly at initialization when the original parameters and stochastic operations are held fixed; added-branch and gate gradients initially vanish, while alpha can receive a gradient. Reuse the computed Fbase value rather than evaluating the branch twice. Avoid consuming random numbers before baseline parameter initialization if checking exact initialization equivalence. Allow alpha to learn without interpreting it as a probability; gate values alone are consequently insufficient evidence of effective branch use.\n\nPreserve the baseline block's normalization, activation, residual ordering, and dilation schedule. Every added convolution must receive left padding of (kernel_size - 1) * dilation and no right-context input. Normalize channels independently at each timestep. Mask padding before convolution and after intermediate transformations that can produce nonzero padding activations, including normalization and convolution biases, and after residual updates. Short branches operate on existing hidden states that may already contain distant context; neither gating nor their receptive field proves forgetting. For stride-one convolution stacks, calculate receptive field as 1 + sum((kernel_size - 1) * dilation) over the applicable path.\n\nKeep the baseline loss, optimizer, sampler, and training controls fixed for the first architecture comparison. Retain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, and exactly 200 batches per epoch. Keep all four dataset routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Train each configuration from scratch; never reuse another trial's checkpoints. One independently trained network/checkpoint per category is mandatory; internal branches are jointly trained components, with no score fusion or separately fitted predictors.\n\nBefore evaluation, record an immutable experiment manifest containing baseline configuration, shorter-window length, checkpoint inspection epochs, stopping rule, practical improvement threshold, and deterministic tie-breaking. A reasonable first shorter-window control keeps the most recent ceil(L/2) interactions, where L is the supplied baseline maximum length. The static control uses the same branches and correction with a learned input-independent channel gate. Both controls help separate shorter-context and additional-capacity explanations; a parameter-matched ungated control and a fixed equal-weight control remain necessary before making stronger mechanistic claims. Do not run an unrestricted factorial sweep. Allocate at most 10,800 GPU-seconds to each of baseline, truncation, static, and content-gated configurations across all categories, counting diagnostics and failures. If baseline timing makes these allocations inadequate, reduce the preregistered comparison set before inspecting validation results and report the missing controls. Equal seeds do not ensure identical negative draws after code changes; preserve sampling streams where feasible and record differences.\n\nSelect only by stipulated four-category macro validation HR@10, retaining the simpler or cheaper configuration on exact ties. Freeze a concrete practical improvement threshold before scores are observed; treat changes below it as inconclusive for added complexity. Use paired bootstrap resampling of the same 200 validation users within each category, averaging category differences equally, for descriptive 95% intervals when per-user outcomes are available. Report category differences and discordant hit counts. Exact McNemar tests may support prespecified category comparisons, with Holm adjustment if multiple hypotheses are tested. Intervals describe uncertainty conditional on the fixed training seed, candidates, and datasets; they do not correct adaptive selection or establish full-catalog performance. Do not modify the host evaluator to obtain additional outputs.\n\nUse matched old-prefix removal or perturbation while preserving recent suffixes, and shuffle gate values across users at matching valid positions, as optional supporting probes. Report sampled-margin diagnostics, parameter counts, GPU-seconds, and throughput; track simulator-specificity and stale-interest rejections qualitatively without inventing labels or claiming causal resolution. Stratification by history length or timestamp gap requires sufficient observed cases and is exploratory. Keep timestamps out of this sequence-only gate because the inference contract does not guarantee timestamp delivery.\n\nSerialize architecture configuration, added parameters, and alpha values; update the embedded predictor writer identically. Preserve log2feats(sequence), item_emb, predict_score(features, items), view.model as the actual nn.Module, all ModelView interfaces, DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Diagnose valid positions only, with explicit metric definitions. Missing item-category metadata must be reported as unavailable, not inferred. Use only public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps; ignore the conflicting generic examples that mention unavailable reviews, ratings, prices, stores, or categories. Read the final nonpadding position using its actual index, never count minus one for left-padded sequences. Follow the supplied baseline's empty-history convention. Freeze one selected checkpoint per category and the matching predictor before host-held-out evaluation.",
    "pseudocode": "Preregister configuration list, checkpoint schedule, budget, stopping rule,\npractical improvement threshold, and deterministic tie-breaking.\n\nFor each configuration and each of the four categories:\n    initialize supplied baseline using seed 42\n    initialize added branches and channel gates; set each alpha = 0\n    for each authorized epoch, at most 200:\n        for exactly 200 baseline-sampled batches:\n            sequence, positive, negative = batch\n            valid = sequence != 0\n            h = item_emb(sequence) * valid[..., None]\n            for block in model.blocks:\n                base = masked_original_causal_update(h, valid)\n                short = masked_short_causal_update(h, valid)\n                if configuration is content_gated:\n                    g = sigmoid(pointwise_gate(h))\n                elif configuration is static_mixture:\n                    g = sigmoid(learned_channel_bias)\n                if configuration has added branches:\n                    h = h + base + alpha * g * (short - base)\n                else:\n                    h = h + base\n                h = h * valid[..., None]\n            optimize unchanged baseline objective on valid target positions\n        evaluate only at preregistered host-compatible checkpoints\n        enforce recorded stopping and GPU-budget rules\n    save eligible checkpoints and matching architecture metadata\n\nSelect the configuration by pinned macro validation HR@10;\napply recorded threshold and tie-breaking rules.\nRetain exactly one selected checkpoint per category.\nEmit predictor with precisely the selected architecture and routing.\n\nInference:\n    features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n    positions = arange(sequence_length)\n    final_index = max(where(sequence != 0, positions, -1), axis=1)\n    handle empty histories with the supplied baseline convention\n    return model.predict_score(features[batch_index, final_index], candidates)\n\nIf paired validation outcomes are supplied:\n    resample user indices within each category, preserving model pairs\n    average the four category HR differences equally per bootstrap replicate\n    report observed macro/category differences, percentile interval,\n    discordant hits, and conditional/exploratory interpretation",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Combines causal content-dependent branch selection with exact baseline-preserving initialization inside the required NextItNet family.",
      "negative": "Selective Kernel Networks, gated convolutions, adaptive NextItNet computation, and zero-initialized residual corrections provide substantial prior art; benchmark adaptation alone offers limited novelty."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Provides a controlled mechanism for studying when recent versus distant sequential context helps recommendation, with feasible static and truncation comparisons.",
      "negative": "Residual states retain older information, and next-item supervision may learn popularity or identity shortcuts instead of current purpose; practical compatibility remains unobserved."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Core network changes are localized and require no external datasets, pretrained weights, or independently fitted models.",
      "negative": "Exact initialization, masking at intermediate layers, branch compute cost, matched controls, diagnostic adaptation, and checkpoint/predictor consistency make a reliable experiment moderately complex."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes residual causal dilated convolutions for sequential recommendation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that content-dependent branch selection improves the supplied benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Frequency-Adaptive Dilated Convolution",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05369",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides inspiration for input-dependent receptive fields."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Evidence concerns vision rather than causal recommendation; it is less direct prior art than receptive-field branch selection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13731",
      "contributions": [
        "Connects item-language representations with sequential recommendation, motivating the alternative text-fusion idea."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its Transformer backbone and initialization assumptions do not directly satisfy this NextItNet experiment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sampled-softmax analysis",
      "link": "https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates competitive ranking supervision with multiple sampled negatives."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish improvements on these categories; sampling, temperature, and false negatives require validation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "SkipRec",
      "link": "https://cdn.aaai.org/ojs/16518/16518-13-20012-1-2-20210518.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies adaptive execution of NextItNet residual blocks."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Adaptive block execution does not establish stale-context rejection or adaptive-dilation benefits.",
        "Reported inference savings do not establish training savings."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "CpRec",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.13139",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies compression and parameter sharing for convolutional sequential recommendation.",
        "Provides evidence that parameter count alone is an inadequate explanation of recommendation quality."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its compression and softmax changes do not isolate content-dependent context selection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Selective Kernel Networks",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06586",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces input-dependent channelwise weighting of receptive-field branches, providing close architectural precedent."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Spatial global pooling must be replaced by per-position or prefix-only conditioning to preserve sequence causality."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "WaveNet",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/html/1609.03499v2",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses gated causal convolutions and residual projections."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Feature gating differs from mixing dilation branches and does not establish recommendation improvements."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "ReZero",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04887",
      "contributions": [
        "Supports zero-initialized trainable residual scaling."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Zeroing an original residual update preserves identity rather than a supplied baseline block; the proposed extension must zero only the added correction."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "RecBole NextItNet implementation",
      "link": "https://www.recbole.io/docs/v1.1.1/_modules/recbole/model/sequential_recommender/nextitnet.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a concrete reference for causal dilations, residual structure, and channelwise normalization."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied corrected host implementation remains authoritative; replacing it wholesale could change unrelated experimental factors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Statistical Significance Tests for Machine Translation Evaluation",
      "link": "https://aclanthology.org/W04-3250/",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides paired resampling methodology adaptable to fixed-user recommendation comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The proposed category-stratified recommendation bootstrap is an adaptation and does not capture training-seed variability or adaptive selection bias."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On the Bias of Offline Evaluation Methods for Recommender Systems",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02263",
      "contributions": [
        "Analyzes limitations of sampled recommendation evaluation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned evaluator; conclusions must remain specific to its candidate protocol."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains selection optimism from repeated model selection."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Ordinary bootstrap intervals and fixed training seeds do not remove this optimism."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions",
      "link": "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v37/blum15.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates limiting adaptive validation feedback and requiring meaningful improvements."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Formal reusable-holdout guarantees require mechanisms absent from the unchanged host evaluator."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
